\subsection{Discussion of Findings}

The audience obeys $\dot N=\Lambda-\mu N$, which has no advertising term. Advertising therefore changes how consumers are spread over the stages, not the size of the audience. The same balance holds in the customer model of \citet{silva2016}, which uses four compartments and numerical simulation. The single rise and fall of the active group in the viral marketing SIR model of \citet{rodrigues2015} is split here into successive peaks in the exposed, engaged and converted groups. Neither study gives a series solution.

For the method, \citet{rafiullah2024} solved a six-compartment model by HPM and checked it against Runge--Kutta. \citet{rangkuti2021} used HAM on a SEIR model and report differences near $10^{-7}$, close to our $3.7\times10^{-8}$, although the models and error measures differ. \citet{sajid2013} and \citet{ratchagar2014} also found that $\hbar$ controls accuracy. Their models are nonlinear, so $\hbar$ can decide convergence, whereas here convergence holds at $\hbar=-1$ and $\hbar$ mainly affects speed. Since HPM is HAM at $\hbar=-1$ \citep{liao2005}, its series here, for constant controls, is the Taylor series, which explains its late-week error. Our order-by-order errors respond to the concern of \citet{aljoufi2024} that HPM accuracy is rarely checked.

The series are polynomials in time, so each order is accurate only on a finite interval, here 10 weeks, and a 52-week horizon would need hundreds of terms. The controls were held constant, the parameters were taken from the literature, and $\hbar$ was chosen by comparison with a numerical solution.

